.TH CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER \- declare buffer for memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER(name, elem_count, elem_size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a uint8_t array sized correctly for a memory pool with the
specified parameters. The buffer can then be passed to
ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(). The size covers the
per-element stride and each element's own status byte, which the pool
keeps in the same buffer after the entries.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name        Variable name for the buffer
elem_count  Number of elements the pool will hold
elem_size   Size of each element in bytes
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Buffer is allocated on stack or in static storage depending on context
Useful for embedded systems or avoiding heap allocation
Buffer is declared with the alignment every entry the pool hands out is
  guaranteed to meet, which is 16 bytes, so it can be handed
  directly to ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() with no
  further alignment considerations
elem_size smaller than sizeof(uintptr_t) is rounded up to fit the
  free-list pointer before the buffer's size is computed, the same
  way ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() itself rounds it, so
  the declared buffer always holds exactly elem_count elements once
  passed to that constructor with the same elem_size
The resulting per-element stride is further rounded up to that same
  alignment, and then to a power of two unless the library and this
  translation unit were both built with -DCCOL_MEMPOOL_COMPACT_LAYOUT=1;
  that setting must match the library's own. It is checked: declaring a
  buffer here references an object the library defines under the name of
  the setting it was built with, so a mismatch is an undefined reference
  to _ccol_mempool_built_with_fast_layout or
  _ccol_mempool_built_with_compact_layout at link time rather than a
  wrongly sized buffer at run time. The two settings differ only for an
  element size that is not already a power of two. Either way the stride
  matches ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() exactly, so every
  element in the resulting pool is aligned for any object type, not just
  the first
One status byte per element is reserved after the elements, in the same
  buffer, so the pool needs no separate allocation of its own
elem_count must be nonzero; rejected at compile time (via a
  _Static_assert) if elem_count is zero, or if elem_count times the
  rounded and aligned elem_size would overflow size_t
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER(my_buffer, 100, 64);
ccol_mempool *mp = ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    my_buffer, sizeof(my_buffer), 64, false, false, NULL, NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer (3)
